## Findings

1. **Dependency Impact (fixable)** — `.github/workflows/rulesets-links.yml` wires `make test-symlinks` and `make test-readme-links` as two separate CI jobs, mirroring Make targets 1:1 (per `techContext.md`: "Pull request CI ... invokes the same Make targets"). Implementation unit 1 adds a new Make target for the Python unit tests and makes `test` depend on it, but no unit in the plan touches the workflow file. PR CI will stay green even if `pick.py` / `refresh.py` regress, because CI never runs `make test` or the new target. Fix: add a job (e.g. `python-tests`) to `rulesets-links.yml` that runs the new Make target, before Build starts.
2. **Convention Compliance (fixable)** — `techContext.md`'s Platform section requires "any rule content or script must hold up under both Windows PowerShell and Mac/Linux Bash." The plan hardcodes the literal invocation `python3 pick.py ...` into `SKILL.md` and all nine Niko spawn sites (units 4 and 5). Windows Python installs commonly expose `python` or the `py` launcher, not `python3`, and nothing in the plan addresses this — Technology Validation only confirms `python3` on the current dev machine. Fix: word the spawn clause so it names the script and flags without hardcoding a platform-specific interpreter binary (e.g. "run this repo's Python 3 interpreter on `pick.py`..."), or otherwise cover the Windows invocation explicitly.
3. **Completeness gap (fixable, minor)** — no unit's file list touches `.gitignore`. The new `make test` unittest-discover target will create `rules/choose-verification-model/__pycache__/` (and per-test-file caches); the existing `.gitignore` only excludes `**/.summem/__pycache__/`, so the first test run leaves the tree dirty. Fix: add a `__pycache__/` (or scoped) entry.
4. **Advisory** — `rulesets/niko/README.md`'s "supplementary rules" bullet list (the three items just above the Subagent Selection tip) enumerates the ruleset's capability skills the same way `illustrate-complexity` is listed. Unit 5 rewrites only the Subagent Selection section; consider adding a `choose-verification-model` bullet there too, for the same reason `illustrate-complexity` has one. Not blocking.
5. **Radical Innovation (advisory, not applied)** — tiers ship 100% null, so the first pick always exits 2 until the operator hand-fills every tier (already accepted in the Pre-Mortem). A more compelling structural option: have `refresh.py` auto-seed `tier_order` and each `tier` from score quantiles on the *first* refresh only (when `tier_order` is empty on input), so the system self-bootstraps into a usable state and the operator's job becomes adjusting boundaries rather than assigning every one from a blank slate. Not applied here — current requirement text locks tiers to a hand-assigned list; this would need requirement-level buy-in before a re-plan, not a Preflight-level edit.

